tic", "target": "Critic", "validation_issues": 0}, {"turn": 8, "ts_utc": "2026-05-14T09:54:06.117962", "env_context": "University: Planning an AI coursework project", "speaker": "Facilitator", "speaker_nature": "facilitator", "reply": "I love that we're converging on emoji buttons — it's such a clean compromise. Critic, you called out the exact implementation fork we needed to resolve, so thanks for pushing us there. So if we're all good with emoji sentiment + branching narrative, should we each sketch out one branch path before our next meeting?", "tone": "positive", "emotion": "encouraging", "target": "Critic", "validation_issues": 1}, {"turn": 9, "ts_utc": "2026-05-14T09:54:11.657278", "env_context": "University: Planning an AI coursework project", "speaker": "Critic", "speaker_nature": "critic", "reply": "Okay, that works. Emoji buttons keep us honest about scope. I'll sketch a branch where the user's choice leads to a dead end — just to make sure we're handling failure states gracefully.", "tone": "neutral", "emotion": "pragmatic", "target": "Facilitator", "validation_issues": 0}, {"turn": 10, "ts_utc": "2026-05-14T09:54:14.616266", "env_context": "University: Planning an AI coursework project", "speaker": "Explorer", "speaker_nature": "explorer", "reply": "Oh wait, Critic — that dead end idea is actually perfect because we could make the \"AI\" part be a hint system that analyzes which emoji they picked before the dead end and nudges them back differently. Keeps the scope tight but still feels smart.", "tone": "positive", "emotion": "excited", "target": "Critic", "validation_issues": 0}]}, "ite": {"avg_tone": 0.0, "avg_reply_length": -4.899999999999999, "actionability_rate": -0.19999999999999998, "reciprocity": 0.19999999999999996, "emotion_entropy": -0.12451124978365335, "question_rate": 0.19999999999999996}, "valid": true, "treatment_error": null, "control_error": null}
